<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=maya-blue">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maya blue</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Azul maya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maya blue is a turquoise or azure blue pigment made by cultures of pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, such as the Maya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, from about 800 CE; it combines <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> dye from the leaves of the anil plant with palygorskite, a natural clay. Found on murals, ceramics, sculptures and codices, it resists weathering: murals at Chichén Itzá, Bonampak and Cacaxtla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> have kept their colour for more than 1,000 years. It was still used in 16th-century convents of colonial Mexico; a recipe to reproduce it was published in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maya-blue.jpg" alt="Maya blue" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonampak_mural._Room_1._Musicians_and_dancers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
